---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="How this website handles data, and how children's work is published."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy notice</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        Story Sprout works with children, so we treat privacy as part of building the site rather
        than as a later addition.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site is a set of static pages. It has no accounts, and it sets no cookies.
      </p>

      <h2>Measurement</h2>
      <p>
        We count how many people find the training sessions and how many go on to sign up,
        because that is how we decide where to spend a small volunteer team's outreach effort.
        We use PostHog for this, and we have configured it narrowly.
      </p>
      <p>What is recorded is a short list of anonymous events — a page being viewed, a session
        button being clicked, a sign-up completing — together with which page it happened on,
        and, if you arrived from one of our outreach emails, which campaign sent you.</p>
      <p>We do not record:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>your name, email address, or organization — the sign-up form's contents are never
          sent to our analytics;</li>
        <li>anything you type, or the contents of any form field;</li>
        <li>your screen, your mouse movements, or your keystrokes — session recording, replay
          and heatmaps are switched off;</li>
        <li>a profile of you across visits.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No cookie or browser storage is used, so nothing identifies you when you come back, and
        there is nothing here to opt into or out of via a banner. We do not build advertising
        audiences and we do not share this data with ad networks. If your browser sends a Global
        Privacy Control or Do Not Track signal, we record nothing at all.
      </p>

      <h2>The webinar sign-up form</h2>
      <p>
        The one place this site collects anything is the training webinar sign-up on the home
        page. It asks for your name, your email address, and — optionally — your school or
        organization, along with the session you chose.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those details are added to a private Google Sheet that only the Story Sprout organizers
        can open, and emailed to them so they can send you the joining link. Nothing is shown
        publicly, there is no account, and the list is not sold, shared, or used for any purpose
        other than running these sessions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google processes the sheet and the notification email on our behalf. To see what we hold
        about you or to have it deleted, reply to the email you receive or contact{' '}
        <a href={siteConfig.parentOrg.url} rel="noopener">{siteConfig.parentOrg.name}</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The form is intended for adults — educators and volunteers. Please do not use it to sign
        a child up.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our analytics records that a sign-up happened and which session it was for. It does not
        receive what you typed.
      </p>
      <h2>Story Sprout Studio</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={siteConfig.platformUrl} rel="noopener">Story Sprout Studio</a> — the place
        children actually write — is a separate application from this website, and the
        following describes it rather than these pages.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Studio is designed to keep a child's work with the adult supervising them. It has
        no child accounts. A project and its revision history are held in a local file that the
        supervising adult controls, and the platform is designed to retain no readable
        server-side copy of it. Requests for illustrations are passed to the model provider
        under that provider's terms.
      </p>

      <h2>How children's work is published</h2>
      <p>Storybooks appear on this site only when both are true:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the item has been approved for publication, and</li>
        <li>its consent status has been confirmed and recorded.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Where a book is published, we do not publish:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>a child's full name, school, contact details, or exact location;</li>
        <li>a child's age inferred from appearance;</li>
        <li>photographs whose permission status is unconfirmed.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Author credits use an approved display name, a group label, or simply "Young authors".
        Alt text describes the artwork and the activity, never the identity of a child.
      </p>
      <p>
        Photographs published here have their EXIF and location metadata removed before
        publication.
      </p>

      <h2>Asking us to remove something</h2>
      <p>
        If you are a parent, guardian, or educator and want a book or image taken down, contact{' '}
        <a href={siteConfig.parentOrg.url} rel="noopener">{siteConfig.parentOrg.name}</a> and we
        will remove it.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <p>
          <strong>Note for the project team.</strong> This page describes the practices the site is
          actually built to follow. Add a direct contact address and have the wording reviewed
          before publishing the site.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .note {
    border: 1px dashed var(--color-border);
    background: var(--color-lavender-soft);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.4rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .note p {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
